rpds-py 2026.9.1 is a Rust extension module built with PyO3. Its Linux
wheels link a static build of the Rust crates below but include only
rpds-py's own license. The crates are the dependencies of the extension
crate rpds-py in the Cargo.lock of the source release that are linked on
Linux; build dependencies and procedural macros run only while building and
are left out. Each directory holds the license files from the crate's
archive on crates.io, and the license after each crate is the one it
declares.

The extension module also links the Rust standard library. Its .comment
section reads "rustc version 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28)". The Rust entry
below is the rust-src archive of that release, with the SHA-256 from its
.sha256 file, and the rust-std-1.99.0 directory holds the license files of
rust-lang/rust and every license file in the archive's library tree. Under
the Rust entry are the standard library's own crates that have code in the
extension module, with the licenses they declare. The crates after it are
the standard library's dependencies from crates.io that have code in the
module, checked against the checksums in its Cargo.lock; their license files
are in rust-std-1.99.0 as well. Code is assigned to crates by the names of
the module's code units and the source paths in its panic messages.

The musllinux wheels, which the Alpine image installs, also include
libgcc_s, which the Rust code needs for unwinding:
rpds_py.libs/libgcc_s-0bf60adc.so.1 (aarch64-musl) and
rpds_py.libs/libgcc_s-f685abf1.so.1 (x86_64-musl). It is licensed under
GPL-3.0-or-later with the GCC Runtime Library Exception 3.1, and both texts
are in the libgcc directory, taken from GCC's repository at release 12.4.0.
Its .comment section reads "GCC: (GNU) 12.4.0". maturin copied it from the
ghcr.io/rust-cross/rust-musl-cross image that built the wheel, and the hex
digits in its name are the start of the original's SHA-256: the
libgcc_s.so.1 of the images that rust-musl-cross built from the commit
below. Those images build GCC with musl-cross-make, which applies its own
patches to GCC, and with the config.mak of rust-musl-cross. The last three
entries below are the corresponding source of the library: the GCC release,
and the two repositories at the commits used. They are attached to the
GitHub release of each version of the image, which the last line of the
image's source file links to.

